   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="syke83">Mr Sykes</name>: Thanks for card. I wrote to <name type="person" key="gold80">Mr G.</name> Telephone to see if all is right. If not try the <name type="geo" key="ID_MISSING">Metropole</name> (<choice>
                            <sic>Fraumunsterstrasse</sic>
                            <corr>Fraumünsterstrasse</corr>
                        </choice>). Can you make three copies? Scribbling in great haste. Please make <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">these two changes</seg> also:</p> <list rend="numbered">
                        <item n="1)">for “The Dicers and thimbleriggers etc . . .” (v. description of racecourse) read “Dicers and thimbleriggers . . . .”, i.e. without “The”.</item>
                        <item n="2)">after “His seacold eyes looked over the empty bay: …” insert after colon “: <hi rend="strike-through">hist it seems</hi> “history is to blame:” &amp; then continue as before “on me and on my words”.</item>
                    </list>
                    <p>There was another correction to make but I cannot remember it.</p>
                    <p>I am rather troublesome, I fear. I wrote the MS in a very legible hand, I think. I know I tried to. Can you <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">cap the limerick</seg>?</p> </div>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <name type="person" key="joyc82">J.J.</name>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">A few days earlier, JJ had sent Sykes the manuscript of “Nestor” to be typed (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LII</name> 413). JJ here requests that Sykes make emendations to two passages that in the published version will be <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">U</name> 2.310 and <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">U</name> 2.246-47.</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">
                    <p>JJ sent four limericks to Sykes in late 1917:</p>
                    <p>5 September 1917 (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LIII</name> 406):</p>
                    <lg>
                        <l>“John Quinn”</l>
                        <l>There’s a donor of lavish largesse</l>
                        <l>Who once bought a play in MS</l>
                        <l>He found out what it all meant</l>
                        <l>By the final instalment</l>
                        <l>But poor Scriptor was left in a mess.</l>
                    </lg> <p>5 September 1917 (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LIII</name> 406):</p>
                    <lg>
                        <l>“Claude Sykes”</l>
                        <l>There is a clean climber called Sykes</l>
                        <l>Who goes scrambling through ditches and dykes</l>
                        <l>To skate on his scalp</l>
                        <l>Down the side of an alp</l>
                        <l>Is the kind of diversion he likes</l>
                    </lg> <p>16 September 1917 (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LIII</name> 406):</p>
                    <lg>
                        <l>“Solomon”</l>
                        <l>There’s a hairyfaced Moslem named Simon</l>
                        <l>Whose tones are not those of a shy man</l>
                        <l>When with cast iron lungs</l>
                        <l>He howls twentyfive tongues –</l>
                        <l>But he’s not at all easy to rhyme on.</l>
                    </lg> <p>17 December 1917 (<name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">LII</name> 410):</p>
                    <lg>
                        <l>“D. L. G.”</l>
                        <l>There’s a George of the Georges named David</l>
                        <l>With whose words we are now night and day fed</l>
                        <l>He cries: I’ll give small rations</l>
                        <l>To all the small nations.</l>
                        <l>Bully God made this world – but I’ll save it.</l>
                        <l>N. N.</l>
                    </lg>
                </note>
            </div>
      </body>
